Every serious bug in the transcription pipeline presents as healthy: socket
OPEN, stream active, callbacks firing, and nothing works. This adds the layer
that makes them visible, and fixes the one that was already biting.

The 90-second lag ratchet. Audio previously went straight to ws.send(), which
never blocks and never refuses — it queues internally, without bound. On a
half-open TCP socket (a Wi-Fi drop with no FIN, where readyState stays OPEN for
the whole retransmit window) minutes of audio pile up invisibly and then flood
Deepgram on recovery, where a 1.25x ingest cap turns it into lag that can only
recover at 0.25x/s and so never does. Replaced with a queue bounded in SECONDS
of audio (10s), draining behind a bufferedAmount high-water mark.

Shed policy: drop from the head, not the tail (dropping the newest keeps you
permanently behind), and evict silence before speech via a cheap RMS gate — in
a real call that usually clears the whole overflow with no lost words. Replay
after a disconnect is capped at 3s; Deepgram's own guidance to buffer while
disconnected is, uncapped, what manufactures the ratchet.

Two-cursor lag is now cumulative ACROSS reconnects. The old counter reset to 0
on every socket open, so it was structurally unable to observe the case it
existed to catch. Watchdog acts on a 5-sample median, with a bucketed
rising-slope guard that trips on a steady climb while the absolute value still
looks fine — and a 3-per-10-minute reset budget so it can't become a reconnect
storm.

Liveness treats readyState as the non-signal it is: separate clocks for dead
capture, dead socket, and sustained digital silence (surfaced, never fatal — a
quiet meeting and a broken mic are byte-identical). Adds the 20ms silence fill
that Deepgram's NET-0001 deadline needs and KeepAlive does not satisfy, tracked
apart from the lag cursors so a long mute can't buy acknowledgement credit.

SessionTimeline makes performance.now() the only timeline source, and detects
sleep from clock divergence rather than powerMonitor (which fires twice on
macOS and sometimes not at all).

Discarded audio is shown, not hidden: a [gap: Ns] marker inline in the
transcript. Markers are stored and rendered but never counted as speech —
speechSegments() keeps them out of every path that counts words, measures talk
ratio, verifies a quote, or prompts a model.

