From 6dafe1b82446996058f57422d97bd81ec054832b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 28 Jul 2026 16:44:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 02/39] =?UTF-8?q?test:=20reproduce=20the=2090s=20lag=20rat?=
 =?UTF-8?q?chet,=20prove=20the=20fix=20(M18=20=C2=A72)?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Drives the real pipeline against a local mock of Deepgram's streaming endpoint
that enforces the 1.25x ingest cap. The cap is the whole point: an
instantly-acknowledging mock would make every test here pass while proving
nothing, because backlog would be free.

Measured, on the real transcription.ts:

- Ratchet reproduced. A 30s backlog replayed the way Deepgram's own
  "buffer while disconnected" guidance implies is still >20s behind after five
  seconds of perfect conditions — catch-up is bounded at 0.25x/s.
- Two 30s outages: 27.0s shed each, two [gap: 27s] markers, queue never over
  the 10s cap, replay to the server ≤3s.
- Recovery after reconnect: lag 3.00s → under 2s at t=4s → 0 at t=12s, at
  exactly the 0.25x/s the cap allows.
- A 20-minute backlog is discarded, not replayed: >1100s shed, ≤3s replayed.
  The guarantee is structural — the queue is bounded in audio-seconds, so the
  buffer cannot exist to be replayed.

Two things the integration run found that unit tests could not:

bufferedAmount is a late signal, not the fix. Measured on loopback: 60s of
audio (1.92 MB) disappeared into a black-holed socket with bufferedAmount
still reading 0 — the kernel absorbed all of it — and it only began reporting
past ~11 MB. At 16kHz mono that blind spot is ~2 minutes, so bufferedAmount
alone would NOT have caught the original bug. What bounds the half-open case
is the liveness watchdog, and the test now asserts that: the dead socket is
detected and rebuilt while readyState still says OPEN. Documented as a table
of which defence covers which failure.

The drift meter reported 25,062,197,486 ppm during a reconnect replay. Frames
are timed by ARRIVAL, so a burst says everything about delivery and nothing
about the clock. The fit now requires a window spanning ≥5s of wall time and
reports 0 otherwise.

Also adds a DEEPGRAM_LISTEN_URL override, which lets the suite point at the
mock and lets a self-hosted Deepgram deployment work without a code change.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01MsbzieENuExjL1yUYc66vr
