From 75143409e31085dbf07e4285a5f42c2723ecbed3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 28 Jul 2026 16:53:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 03/39] feat: always-record setting, and fix auto-start only
 ever firing once
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two things, both about calls starting themselves.

"Always record the other party" (Settings → Recording & consent). Once on, the
buyer side transcribes automatically from the start of every call with no
per-call consent step.

It does NOT bypass consent — it records one. Each call still gets a real,
timestamped ConsentRecord with method 'pre-agreed', so sanitizeConsent's hard
invariant (recordOtherParty only ever true alongside status 'consented') is
completely untouched, and a saved call stays honest about how consent was
obtained. It is gated on the existing master switch, so that switch still
removes the capability outright. Off by default, because it is only defensible
when the rep genuinely has a standing basis — a recorded-line notice, a signed
agreement, or a one-party jurisdiction — and only they can know that. The
Settings copy says exactly that rather than implying the app handles it.

Buyer capture now auto-enables once the session is live, one attempt per call.
getDisplayMedia wants a recent user gesture and an ambient-detected call may
have none, so when it fails the existing recovery banner takes over — its "Try
again" IS a gesture. Retrying in a loop would spam a prompt that cannot succeed.

The auto-start bug. autoStartedRef was set in three places and never reset, so
only the FIRST auto-start in the Live view's lifetime ever worked. Every later
detected call hit the latch and vanished: the banner's "Start transcribing"
button did nothing, and ambient auto-start reported a failure it could not
explain. With auto-start-on-open enabled it was worse — that burned the latch
at launch, killing every detection for the rest of the session.

The shared guard now clears when a call ends. Auto-start-on-open keeps its own
separate latch, since it means "start when the app opens", not "start after
every call". The detection request is also no longer consumed before it is
acted on, so one arriving while the previous call is still tearing down is
honoured once status settles instead of being swallowed.

Also bounds the detected-call banner: it was an unwrapped single row carrying a
variable-length app name, so it clipped its own button on a narrow window.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01MsbzieENuExjL1yUYc66vr
