MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

§3.1 said to evaluate WerdoxDev/application-loopback before writing an addon,
so that came first. It is published as `loopback-capture`, MIT, an N-API port
of Microsoft's own WASAPI sample, declares napi_versions [9] (ABI-stable, so
one binary spans Node and Electron), ships a prebuilt .node, and already
exposes BOTH per-process and system loopback.

It still does not collapse days 1-2, because the parts it provides are the easy
parts. startSystemAudio(callback) takes no device argument, so it cannot be
pointed at the eCommunications endpoint — which is the entire bug. It has no
Teams workaround, no IMMNotificationClient, and hardcodes 48kHz stereo where
STT wants 16k mono. Recommendation: use as reference, write our own.

Confirmed the Teams bug directly rather than taking it on faith:
Windows-classic-samples#414 is still open, both INCLUDE and EXCLUDE tree modes
fail, device loopback captures Teams fine. So the fallback path is mandatory.

Landed the piece that needs no native code and can be tested here: the
auto-switch heuristic. Windows answers two questions at once — is the app
producing sound (IAudioMeterInformation peak) and are we receiving any of it
(RMS of the PCM we got). Both true together is not a quiet meeting, it is a
broken capture path, and it is the same measurement the session-health liveness
probe already makes. Sustained 2s → switch to device loopback and log why.

Deliberate choices, each with a test: an unreadable meter never switches
(absence of evidence is not evidence of absence — treating null as "playing"
would send every quiet call to the fallback); the switch is one-way (device
loopback captures everything process loopback does, so a return trip buys
nothing and risks oscillating mid-call); and a quiet meeting must not trigger
it, which is the false positive that would make the feature worse than useless.

docs/windows-capture.md carries the design, the endpoint-role explanation, the
addon implementation notes, the library evaluation, and one finding that
changes this phase's shape: a native addon delivers buyer PCM to the MAIN
process while the mic stays in the renderer, so the two sides stop sharing an
AudioContext. Merging them means reconciling two independent clock domains —
a jitter buffer plus real drift correction, exactly the machinery the current
single-context design was chosen to avoid. That is not in the original
estimate and is the risky part.

The addon itself is not written: it cannot be compiled or tested from a Linux
container, and cannot reach the Windows CI runner while pushes are rejected
with 403. Several hundred lines of untestable COM code would only be honestly
describable as unverified.

