From ed69a7e268eef9d68e5455229bace1e3a4e17c30 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 28 Jul 2026 17:19:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 06/39] fix: an auto-started call now ends and saves itself
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Reported: after a call is over the app keeps recording, and the rep has to
open it and press Stop before anything is saved. Both auto-start paths were
broken, for different reasons, and the consequence in each case is the worst
one available — the call is only written to disk when the session CLOSES, so
"nobody pressed Stop" meant the call was never saved at all. The rep believes
it was recorded and there is nothing there.

Ambient detection (M15) already knew. The FSM decides a call has ended and
emits capture-ended with reason 'call-ended', every UI surface updates to say
"not capturing" — and the microphone and Deepgram session keep running,
because only the MANUAL stops went through stopCapture(), which is the thing
that does both halves. The one path nobody drives by hand was the one that
never stopped anything. handleDetectorEvent now broadcasts the stop request
for any end reason it did not originate itself.

The app-name detector (the default path) has no end signal at all — it only
ever announces that a call STARTED. So it needed one, and the honest signal
available is the transcript: not audio silence, which the liveness probe
already reports within ten seconds and which says nothing about a call being
over, but a sustained absence of transcribed WORDS. Five minutes, deliberately
generous: real calls contain long pauses — someone is put on hold, pulls up a
document — and truncating a live conversation is worse than recording a few
extra minutes of nothing.

Armed only for calls the app started itself. A rep who pressed Start is
present and in control; timing them out mid-thought would be a surprise rather
than a rescue. It also ends a detected "call" that never produced a single
word, which otherwise recorded an empty room until the app was closed.

When the app ends a call on its own it says so — a call that stops with no
explanation reads as a crash. The notice resets at the start of the next call
so it can never describe the wrong one.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01MsbzieENuExjL1yUYc66vr
