Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

§4.1 — the overlay is now excluded from screen capture.

This is the prerequisite for every other Phase 4 feature, not a hardening pass
to do afterwards. The overlay floats above every other window by design, which
is exactly what makes it leak, and the failure mode is not cosmetic: a
battlecard naming the prospect's competitor rendered on the prospect's screen
ends the deal. Competitors market "invisible during screen shares" as a
headline feature, which tells you overlay leakage is a live problem in this
category rather than a theoretical one.

setContentProtection(true) covers both platforms — NSWindowSharingNone on
macOS, SetWindowDisplayAffinity(WDA_EXCLUDEFROMCAPTURE) on Windows 10 2004+,
falling back to WDA_MONITOR on older builds (the window is blanked in the
capture rather than omitted — uglier, still not a leak). Applied
unconditionally with no toggle: a switch here is one mis-click away from the
failure it exists to prevent. Our own buyer capture is unaffected, since
getDisplayMedia is opened for audio and the video track is stopped immediately.

docs/screen-share-safety.md carries the per-app verification matrix, all of it
marked NOT YET RUN. Each conferencing app captures differently — window vs
display capture, DXGI duplication, browser getDisplayMedia, ScreenCaptureKit —
so a pass on one proves little about the others, and every cell has to be
checked from the RECEIVING side. A screenshot of the sharer's own screen
proves nothing. That needs two machines and cannot be done from here.

Design: both floating popups now share one glass material.

The call-detected banner was a rectangle with lightly rounded corners; it is
now a true capsule, which is the shape that reads as "a notification" rather
than "a panel". The ambient overlay keeps a larger radius, because a taller
card wants a generous squircle where a single-row bar wants a capsule.

Both draw on a new .glass-hud utility so they can never drift apart — a rep
sees both within the same second, and two different-looking floating panels
read as two different apps. Three things make it a material rather than a grey
box: saturate() on the backdrop (blur alone renders what is behind as flat mud;
pushing saturation back up is the whole illusion), a specular top edge where
physical glass would catch light, and a large soft shadow that lifts it off the
