Subject: [PATCH 08/39] =?UTF-8?q?feat:=20two-tier=20cue=20architecture=20?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=E2=80=94=20LLM=20cues=20off=20the=20interrupt=20path=20(M18=20?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=C2=A74.3)?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Phase 0 found the violation §4.3 forbids: the model's cue and the
deterministic pace cue went through the same emit(), competing for one
interrupt slot. So an LLM suggestion could take over the rep's attention
1.5-2.5s after the moment it described — past the threshold where an
interruption costs more attention than it returns — and, worse, a deterministic
cue and a model cue could each suppress the other.

Now two genuinely independent channels:

INTERRUPT — deterministic only. Keeps the strict one-at-a-time slot, the
cooldown and the auto-dismiss, because interrupting mid-sentence is expensive
and has to earn its place.

SIDE RAIL — model-generated. Deliberately none of that machinery: no single
slot, no cooldown, no auto-dismiss stealing it away mid-read, no check against
whether a deterministic cue is showing. A suggestion cannot delay, replace or
suppress an interrupt, and an interrupt cannot suppress a suggestion. That
independence IS the architecture. Bounded instead at three, newest first, with
a 90s TTL, because advice about a moment that has passed is noise wearing the
clothes of help.

The split is structural, not conventional: tierFor() derives the tier from the
kind, so no call site can put a model cue on the interrupt path, and an
unrecognised kind defaults to the side rail — guessing wrong in that direction
only costs an interruption the rep did not earn.

One coupling was easy to miss and is now removed: callBrain() used to return
early if a cue was visible or the interrupt cooldown was active. Those guards
belong to the interrupt channel; applying them to the model call meant a
deterministic "slow down" silently suppressed the entire side rail for the
whole cooldown, re-coupling exactly what the split separates.

The rail is styled to stay quiet — no slide-in, no countdown, it does not take
the corner the interrupt uses, and it is an aria-live="polite" log rather than
a status region, since an announced-on-change region is an auditory interrupt.

Also lands §1.7: measured turn-end → cue-rendered latency, tracked per tier
with nearest-rank p50/p95 (never interpolated — every published number must be
a latency that genuinely occurred), plus max, since a p95 can look healthy
while hiding a 9-second tail. Measuring the tiers separately is the point:
quoting the fast number for both is the dishonesty this prevents. Real p50/p95
figures need real call data, so acceptance criterion 8 is instrumented here,
not yet answered.

