Subject: [PATCH 09/39] =?UTF-8?q?fix:=20QA=20pass=20on=20the=20live=20scre?=
 =?UTF-8?q?en=20=E2=80=94=20four=20real=20defects,=20three=20of=20them=20m?=
 =?UTF-8?q?ine?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Reviewing §4.3's own output rather than assuming it was fine.

The cue and the suggestion rail could overlap. Both were absolutely positioned
against the right edge — cue at bottom-4 with no fixed height, rail ending at
bottom-20 — so a cue that wrapped to three lines slid straight under the
lowest suggestion, and the cue's higher z-index meant the suggestion was the
one that vanished. Fixed by construction rather than by tuning the offsets:
both now live in one bottom-anchored column, interrupt lowest and nearest the
eye. They stay logically independent — a suggestion still cannot delay,
replace or suppress an interrupt — they simply can no longer collide. Picking
offsets that happen not to overlap works right up until the text changes.

AUTO_DISMISS_MS existed twice, in useLiveCues and in CueCard, with a comment
saying it was kept in sync by hand. Two copies of a number drift the moment
either is tuned, and the symptom is subtle: a countdown bar that finishes early
or hangs full while the cue disappears underneath it. Now exported from one
place.

The stack clipped its own children's shadows. overflow-hidden on the column cut
the glass shadow off flush at the card's border — the exact bug the detection
overlay already carries a comment about, repeated. Removed.

Per-turn console tracing shipped to users. Three logs on the live-cue hot path
fired on every client turn, so a 40-minute call wrote hundreds of lines into
the packaged app's console and did the template-literal work to produce them.
Compiled out of production builds now.

One thing checked and deliberately left alone: the cue card's accent moved from
a border-colour utility to an inset ring, because .glass-hud declares its own
border and a `border-warning/40` utility next to it is a specificity race whose
outcome depends on stylesheet order. A ring is additive and cannot lose.

Design: the interrupt cue now uses the same .glass-hud material as the rail and
the two popups, with a stronger tinted ring for weight. One material, two
volumes — so the live screen reads as one system rather than a glass rail
sitting beside a flat card.

Also verified, and already correct, so untouched: prefers-reduced-motion and
:focus-visible are both properly handled app-wide.

