From ad58dc8ce50d588eece8621062a72294ddb210ee Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 28 Jul 2026 18:35:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 10/39] =?UTF-8?q?feat:=20instant=20post-call=20brief=20+?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=20follow-up=20email=20to=20the=20clipboard=20(M18=20=C2=A74.6)?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A call ends and the rep already has a brief, the next steps and a
ready-to-send follow-up email on their clipboard, with nothing to press.
Gong's equivalent takes 20-30 minutes to arrive. Mostly a repackaging of the
existing summary pipeline, which is the point — the best ratio of
perceived-new-product to actual-new-code in the milestone.

The one decision that mattered: the clipboard write happens in MAIN, not the
renderer. navigator.clipboard.writeText needs the document focused, and think
about when this fires — the call just ended, so the rep is still in Zoom
looking at the goodbye screen. Our window is not focused, and that is the one
moment we can be certain of it. A renderer-side write would fail silently in
precisely the case the feature exists for. Electron's main-process clipboard
has no focus requirement.

Smaller calls, each with a reason:

- A call under 25 words is refused outright. Spending a request, and putting a
  confidently-worded brief about nothing on the rep's clipboard, is worse than
  doing nothing.
- An empty email body is treated as failure rather than shipped. The whole
  promise is "it is already on your clipboard"; quietly putting a blank draft
  there is worse than saying nothing happened.
- The clipboard text puts the email LAST, after a separator. The rep's usual
  move is to paste the lot into their mail client and delete everything above
  it — a two-second edit only if the email is the tail.
- The save banner says the clipboard changed. An unannounced clipboard write is
  indistinguishable from losing whatever the rep had copied.

Defaults OFF, which is a deliberate divergence from §4.6's "no click". This
codebase already has an explicit, documented convention that anything sending
the transcript to the model on every saved call is opt-in, because it is a real
behaviour and cost change on the rep's own key. "No click" is satisfied where
it matters — once enabled there is no per-call friction at all — and opting a
rep into recurring spend without asking once is not what that phrase can
reasonably mean.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
