From 05a2ef6f63588233424c1dc2a7b0513de8eab7fd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 28 Jul 2026 18:43:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 11/39] =?UTF-8?q?feat:=20deterministic=20battlecards=20+?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=20a=2032-card=20starter=20library=20(M18=20=C2=A74.4)?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Phrase triggers matched against the rolling PARTIAL transcript, which is where
the ~400ms budget comes from: ASR partial ~300ms + match ~50ms + render ~50ms.
Waiting for a finalized turn would add a second or more, by which point the
moment to answer the objection has gone.

The hard part is not matching. It is matching against a buffer that keeps
being handed to you with the same words in it — interim results grow a
sentence a word at a time, and a finalized turn sits in the window for seconds
afterwards, so a naive matcher fires one objection's card dozens of times. A
per-trigger 60s cooldown and normalization handle that.

A real bug the tests caught: normalization replaced ALL punctuation with a
space, so "you're a startup" became "you re a startup" and matched neither
"you are a startup" nor "youre a startup" — a card written for a contraction
could never fire on the contraction. Apostrophes now join while other
punctuation still separates.

Padding is what makes a naive includes() behave like a word-boundary match:
searching " arm " inside " we need an alarm " correctly fails, where
includes('arm') would fire a contract-terms card at someone discussing sirens.

Two judgement calls, both tested:

Battlecards take the RAIL, not the interrupt channel — even though they are
deterministic and just as fast as the pace cue. Being fast is necessary to
earn the right to interrupt; it does not create the reason to. A battlecard is
reference material the rep consults, not a nudge about something they are
doing wrong this second.

Matching is skipped while the REP is talking. A rep restating an objection
back to the buyer should not fire a card at themselves.

That distinction also corrected the latency buckets from §4.3: they now
measure how a cue was PRODUCED (deterministic vs model), not where it renders.
Bucketing by channel would have averaged a 400ms battlecard match into the
same figure as a 2s completion — quoting the fast number for the slow thing,
which is exactly what measuring separately exists to prevent.

The library ships 32 curated triggers rather than an empty authoring screen:
the library is the head of this feature, an authoring UI is the tail. Two
rules held throughout — every line has to be readable mid-call (if the rep has
to stop listening to parse it, it cost them the conversation it was meant to
save), and patterns favour precision over recall, because a card that fires
when it should not trains the rep to ignore the rail, and an ignored surface
is a dead one. Both are enforced by tests, along with no duplicate phrases
across triggers.

