Date: Tue, 28 Jul 2026 19:38:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 12/39] =?UTF-8?q?feat:=20lock=20the=20Electron=20fuses=20+?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=20ship=20CSP=20as=20a=20header=20(M18=20=C2=A75.3,=20part=201)?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The fuses are the highest-severity item in the milestone, and they were not
set at all.

Without them a signed, notarized CallRise AI is a general-purpose code
execution primitive that inherits the app's own permissions:

  ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE=1 ".../CallRise AI" -e '<anything>'

runs arbitrary JavaScript as us. macOS granted the microphone to THIS BUNDLE,
so anything running inside it inherits that grant. For an app that exists to
hold mic access — and, with consent, the other party's audio — that is the
difference between a bug and a wiretap someone else can point at your
customers. The mic permission is also the one thing an attacker cannot quietly
obtain on their own: prompting for it draws attention, borrowing ours does not.

scripts/apply-fuses.js runs as afterPack, deliberately before signing since
flipping a fuse invalidates a signature. RunAsNode and NodeCliInspect off (the
second would otherwise attach a debugger to main and achieve the same thing
more comfortably); asar integrity validation and OnlyLoadAppFromAsar on. Those
two are a pair — integrity-validating an archive nobody is forced to load is
worth nothing, so enabling either alone leaves the door open.

CSP now ships as a real response header, not only the <meta> tag. They are not
equivalent: several directives are ignored via meta (frame-ancestors among
them) and a meta tag only applies once parsing reaches it. This matters because
transcripts are attacker-influenced text — the person on the other end of the
call chooses the words rendered in this window — as is every model response
derived from them. connect-src 'self' is the load-bearing directive: the
renderer makes no network calls at all, so injected script has nowhere to send
what it steals. Packaged builds only; applying it in dev would break Vite's HMR
client, and breaking npm run dev to harden a build that does not exist yet is a
bad trade.

Audited rather than assumed, in docs/security-audit.md:

- The one credential in the bundle decodes to role: anon, ref fphvsuvpskqwkcpiocfz,
  exp 2036-06-29. No service_role key anywhere in src/, .env excluded from the
  package. That is the key designed to be public — which is precisely why the
  RLS audit is not optional: it is extractable in thirty seconds, so every
  guarantee that one user cannot read another's calls rests on RLS alone.
- Zero innerHTML / dangerouslySetInnerHTML occurrences.
- RLS policy shape in the repo is right: every policy user_id = auth.uid(), no
  org_id at all (so the user_metadata tenant-escape cannot exist here), no
  bare auth.role() = 'authenticated' predicate, WITH CHECK on every write, and
  no views (which run as owner and would leak).

The doc keeps "verified here" and "you must run" strictly apart, because an
unrun check is not a pass. The live-database queries and the cross-tenant
PostgREST attack are written out ready to paste — the UI adds its own filters
and would mask a missing policy completely, so inspecting is not testing.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
