From 1fb7204c5370e45d4cb820180b9c860e3c0f292b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 28 Jul 2026 19:44:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 13/39] =?UTF-8?q?feat:=20durable=20consent=20gate=20?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=E2=80=94=20capture=20cannot=20start=20without=20a=20record=20o?=
 =?UTF-8?q?n=20disk=20(=C2=A75.3,=20criterion=2011)?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Buyer capture was already triple-gated: a renderer check, a one-shot arm in
main, and the Settings master switch. But all three were PROCESS STATE. The
consent record itself lived in renderer memory for the length of the call and
only reached disk when the call was saved.

That distinction is the whole point of the criterion. "Capture cannot start
without consent" has to be provable from something that outlives the process
claiming it, because the failure being guarded against is a banner reading
"recording in progress" while audio is already being written — and a flag in
memory cannot tell that story afterwards, to anyone, ever.

Consent is now written to disk BEFORE capture is armed, and both the arm and
the grant read it back from disk. Main never asks the renderer whether consent
exists; it looks.

- sanitizeConsent runs on write AND on read, so the same hard invariant as a
  saved call applies to this file too: recordOtherParty is only honoured
  alongside an explicit 'consented' status. A hand-edited file claiming consent
  it never had collapses on read.
- Only the sanitized record is written — an unrecognised method, or any extra
  field the renderer attached, never reaches disk.
- Scoped to a session id, so consent recorded on call A cannot authorise call
  B. That is the case that actually matters: a rep who consented, hung up, and
  started another call without being asked again.
- Turning consent off DELETES the grant rather than merely failing to renew it.
- Cleared on every app start, so a record left by a crash mid-call cannot
  authorise the next launch's first call.
- Written synchronously, because the renderer calls it inside the click that
  opens getDisplayMedia — an async round-trip there would spend the user
  activation and the capture prompt would never appear. Same reason
  loopback:arm has always been sendSync.

Standing consent ("Always record the other party") takes the identical path:
the gate does not care where consent came from, only that a record for this
call exists on disk.

20 tests, including every confident-looking object a compromised or buggy
renderer might send — a truthy-but-not-true flag, a declined status paired with
the capture flag, a file with no session, unparseable contents.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
