From 3a22a779269cf1c80ad944b83e4e4c1414b714f7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 28 Jul 2026 19:50:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 14/39] =?UTF-8?q?fix:=20a=20segment's=20identity=20is=20(c?=
 =?UTF-8?q?hannel,=20speaker),=20never=20a=20bare=20integer=20(=C2=A75.1)?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Found in Phase 0 recon, and it silently corrupts saved data rather than
crashing, which is why it survived this long.

`speaker` alone means two different things depending on how the call was being
captured. In mono it is a diarized guess; in multichannel it is the channel
index. So "speaker 0" is the rep after buyer capture starts, and whoever
diarization happened to label first before that — and a call that switched
mid-way saves both under the same number with nothing to tell them apart.

Everything downstream then quietly believes them to be one person: turns merge
across the switch into a single sentence, talk ratio counts the buyer's words
as the rep's, and the coaching scorecard grades a conversation that never
happened. Nothing errors. The numbers are just wrong.

Segments now carry the capture channel alongside the speaker, and identity is
the PAIR — exposed as speakerKey() ("ch0/spk0", "mono/spk0"), a string
specifically so nobody can compare the integer half on its own by accident.
groupWords and mergeSegments key on sameSpeaker(), so a mono turn can never
merge into a channel turn and channel 0 can never merge into channel 1.

The field is optional and absent on mono, so every existing saved call keeps
working and reads exactly as it did before. sanitizeSegments accepts only 0 or
1 and drops anything else, like every other field crossing that boundary.

STILL OPEN, and worth being explicit since this is only half of §5.1:
multichannel mode sets `multichannel` INSTEAD of `diarize`, so the buyer
channel gets no speaker separation at all and three people on the far end
collapse into one "Buyer". The fix is to run diarize on the loopback channel
only — the mic is one known person by construction — but that changes live
labelling, which I cannot verify without a real call. The ambiguity bug above
was the half that corrupts data on disk; this half only under-resolves.

