The failure this prevents is a TRUST failure, not a crash:
"I unplugged my headset and it kept recording the laptop mic."

It happens because deviceId: "default" does not mean "follow the system
default". Chromium resolves it once, at acquisition, and pins it
(crbug 40199570) — so when the headset goes away the track does not end, it
quietly continues on whatever the OS moved to. The rep believes they are
recording a headset in a quiet room; they are recording a laptop mic in an
open-plan office, and nothing on screen says otherwise.

Three properties of `devicechange` make it unusable directly, all handled here
rather than at the call site:

It fires spuriously — Electron emits it on first device open, before anything
has changed, so a naive handler reacquires the mic seconds after starting the
very call it was meant to protect. Hence a 2s startup grace, which DROPS the
snapshot rather than queueing it: whatever it showed is the state we started
in, not a change.

It fires in bursts — one USB headset produces several events as its input and
output endpoints appear, so acting on the first means acting on a
half-enumerated list. Hence a 400ms debounce.

It carries no payload — so the only honest signal is a diff of the enumerated
list against the last one we settled on, re-baselined after each report so one
change is never reported twice.

"The device in use disappeared" is returned as its own case, checked first and
prioritised over any additions in the same event, because it is the only change
that means the recording is no longer what the rep thinks it is.

Also adds the generation counter reacquisition needs. Device events arrive in
bursts and getUserMedia is async, so two attempts can legitimately be in flight
— and two overlapping calls produce two live tracks feeding one graph. That is
doubled audio: every word transcribed twice, and a talk ratio claiming the rep
spoke for 200% of the call. Only the newest generation may install its stream.

18 tests. Pure policy, no Web Audio — the wiring into the recorder still has to
happen, and that part needs a real device to verify.

