Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The research disagrees with itself here, and the disagreement IS the design
constraint. Talk ratio is the single most-loved Gong metric — and as a live
interrupt it has the weakest evidence of any real-time feature: actionable, and
distracting exactly when the rep can least afford it. The form that survives
contact with a real call is a passive meter.

So nothing here interrupts. The meter changes colour and the number moves; that
is the whole interaction. A rep talking too much finds out by glancing, not by
being told mid-sentence — which would itself interrupt the thing being measured.

Three decisions, each tested:

"Uninterrupted" is measured by TURNS, not by silence. A pause for breath is not
the buyer speaking, and resetting on it would clear the timer every few seconds
and never register the monologue this exists to catch. Any turn from anyone
else resets it — that is the buyer getting in, which is the thing that needs to
happen.

The ratio is null, not zero and not 0.5, until there are enough words to mean
anything. A gauge reading "100% you" because the rep said hello first is a
gauge the rep stops believing by minute two.

Only one direction is ever flagged. A discovery call SHOULD be buyer-heavy, so
there is no "you are listening too much" state — a meter that complains about
listening is worse than no meter.

Deterministic throughout: word counts and timestamps from the turn buffer that
already exists. No extra ASR, no model call, no network. Works offline and
cannot be wrong in an interesting way.

