Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Phase 6 from a different angle. Treating it as "produce installers" made it
look blocked on the 403; the useful framing is "make everything that produces
installers correct and ready", and the largest piece of that needs no CI at all.

--diagnose prints one block of text and exits without opening a window. It
exists because every hard bug this app has had shares a shape: invisible on the
developer's machine, unreproducible on the user's. A tester who can paste one
block turns "the buyer side isn't working" — which could be a permission, an
endpoint, a consent gate, a missing addon or a lag ratchet — into a line that
says which.

Two rules hold throughout, and the tests enforce both.

NEVER CLAIM A CHECK RAN WHEN IT DID NOT. The report says the per-process
capture path is "not built" rather than naming the path the code intends to
have one day, because a row reading "ok" because nothing tested it is worth
less than nothing on the exact machine this is meant to debug. On Windows it
states outright that render endpoints are not enumerated and that a headset set
as Default Communication Device is still the known cause of silent capture.

NEVER PRINT A SECRET. API keys are reported as set/not set. Tested by putting a
real-looking key in the environment and asserting the value does not appear —
this report is meant to be pasted into a bug thread by a stranger.

Also lands acceptance criterion 6, the channel self-test. Buyer capture
interleaves two mono sources into one stereo stream and everything downstream
trusts that layout absolutely: "channel 1 said it" is how the app knows the
BUYER said it. Get it backwards and nothing errors — the transcript simply
attributes every one of the rep's own words to the prospect and the scorecard
grades the wrong person. So a known tone goes down one channel at a time,
through the REAL interleaver rather than a copy that could agree with a bug,
and the energy is measured coming out. 18 tests, including the three ways this
fails silently in production: swapped channels, a dropped channel, and both
sources mixed into both (which sounds fine and destroys attribution).

