From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 28 Jul 2026 20:46:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 20/39] =?UTF-8?q?feat:=20commitment=20extractor=20+=20plai?=
 =?UTF-8?q?n-English=20custom=20trackers=20(M18=20=C2=A74.7,=20=C2=A74.8)?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The last two Phase 4 features.

§4.7 — commitments as the PRIMARY artifact, not a bullet buried in a summary.
Gong extracts much the same information and files it inside a summary, where it
is something you could look up if you remembered to. A checklist that persists
across every call with an account is a different object: the thing you open
before the next call, and the thing that says "you said you'd send the SOC 2
report and you haven't". Same extraction, better product decision.

The owner split is what makes it work — "send the security docs" and "they'll
loop in their CISO" are both commitments, and a blended list has to be re-read
every time to work out which ones are yours.

Malformed entries are DROPPED, never repaired. A commitment with a guessed
owner is worse than no commitment: the rep acts on the list without re-reading
the call, and chasing a prospect for something you promised is a specific and
memorable way to lose trust. Dates get the same treatment — 2026-02-31 parses
happily in JS and silently becomes March 3rd, so the date is round-tripped and
dropped unless it survives, rather than putting a due date on a day nobody
mentioned.

§4.8 — a rep types "tell me when someone mentions procurement" and has a
working trigger on the next call.

The queue removal is the point, not the convenience. Gong's Smart Trackers go
unused at most organisations not because the feature is bad but because
creating one is somebody else's job, so the person who knows what to track and
the person able to add it are never the same person.

The model writes the trigger; sanitizeGeneratedTrigger decides whether it is
allowed to exist, holding it to exactly the same standard as the curated
starter library — same length ceilings, same precision floor. A custom card
firing on every other sentence trains the rep to ignore the rail, and once the
rail is ignored the curated cards die with it. Ids are namespaced `custom-` and
de-duplicated, because an id the model chose could collide with a starter
trigger and silently shadow it. A test drives the generated trigger through the
REAL matcher, so the validation is not theatre.

Both treat their input as untrusted: model output shaped by a user's free text
is two layers of untrusted at once, and the failure that matters is not a crash
but a plausible-looking object that is quietly wrong.

