 =?UTF-8?q?as=20a=20sleep-detection=20speed=20hint=20(M18=20=C2=A75.2)?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

SleepDetector's clock-divergence check stays the actual mechanism —
powerMonitor fires 'resume' twice on macOS and sometimes not at all on Linux,
so it can never be trusted as the detector itself. But it's a good speed hint:
without it, a resume is only noticed on the next 1Hz health tick. Wiring
'resume' to force an immediate tick shrinks that to the width of the event;
firing twice is harmless since the second call sees the divergence already
collapsed to zero and no-ops.
