Runs the real pipelines directly (disposable measurement harnesses, deleted
after use — not part of the test suite) to get actual numbers rather than
assertions, per §9's "Measured numbers for every acceptance criterion. Not
assertions.":

- Criterion 1: forced two 30s drops against the real transcription.ts +
  1.25x-ingest-cap mock — lag recovers to 0s, crosses under 2s at 4014ms
  (< the 10s requirement), 54s of gap correctly reported.
- Criterion 2: lag capped at 3.9s (under the 5s shed threshold) still trips
  the rising-slope guard's reset action, proving it catches the ratchet
  before absolute lag looks alarming.
- Criterion 6: real per-channel tone through the production interleaver —
  0.354 RMS (exactly the expected value for a 0.5-amplitude sine) on the
  right channel only, in both stereo and mono layouts.
- Criterion 8: the tier classification is exhaustively proven (every CueKind,
  not a sample); the deterministic matcher's own compute cost is measured at
  p50 0.03-0.13ms, p95 0.06-0.19ms — confirming it is nowhere near the 500ms
  budget and the budget is spent elsewhere (ASR + render, both unmeasurable
  without a live connection/renderer).
- Criteria 11 and 12: cited by their real, current test counts (20/20, 41/41)
  rather than "tests pass".

Criterion 3 (main-thread-stall isolation) is marked architecturally addressed
but not independently end-to-end proven, with the reason stated plainly: a
faithful test needs a real Chromium Worker, which means adding Playwright/E2E
infra — a new dependency and test category, flagged here rather than added
unilaterally.

Criteria 4, 5, 7, 9, 10, 13 are honestly deferred to environments this
container doesn't have (real Windows hardware, a live Supabase project, a
live LLM API key, two machines for screen-share verification, Windows CI
reachability) — cross-referenced to the existing docs that already track
each one rather than restated inconsistently.
