thread-independence.test.ts spins up a real worker_threads.Worker (a genuine
separate OS thread, not a simulation) ticking every 20ms, then synchronously
busy-blocks the test's own thread for a real 5000ms with nothing that could
yield. Measured: 248 of an expected ~250 ticks landed inside the blocked
window, with a max gap of 21ms — the worker's cadence was practically
undisturbed by the other thread being fully blocked for five real seconds.

This is the platform guarantee the whole §1.4 ring/pump design leans on:
moving audio delivery onto a real Worker only helps if a separate OS thread
genuinely keeps running while another thread stalls. No new dependency —
worker_threads is built into Node, unlike the Playwright/E2E harness a fully
faithful Chromium-renderer proof would need (that remains a flagged, not
silently added, gap — see docs/acceptance-criteria.md).

Updates docs/acceptance-criteria.md's criterion 3 entry with the real numbers
and moves it from "architecturally addressed" to "measured", while being
precise about what this substitution does and does not cover (Node threads,
not literally the DOM Worker + Electron renderer combination in production).
