From 7f9aa2271571102b0675fa5c1a401bea4f473e4d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 03:05:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 33/39] docs: note the further-improvements sweep's
 dead-feature fix in CLAUDE.md
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The M18 entry already described the monologue meter, commitment extractor,
and custom trackers as shipped — which was the built-and-tested half of the
truth. Adds a sentence recording that a further-improvements sweep caught
all three being unreachable by an actual user, and that they're now wired
end-to-end, so a future session reading this doc gets the honest sequence
rather than a claim that was quietly wrong for a while.
---
 CLAUDE.md | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/CLAUDE.md b/CLAUDE.md
index acf923c..9c63594 100644
--- a/CLAUDE.md
+++ b/CLAUDE.md
@@ -35,7 +35,7 @@ Milestones completed:
 - **CRM Phase 5, further steps.** ✅ Builds on Phase 5 Step 1 (manual risk assessment): deals now keep a **capped history of past risk assessments** (not just the latest), shown as a collapsible timeline on the deal detail view; the follow-up dashboard gained a **`risk-stale`** tier flagging a deal whose risk read is over 30 days old as due for a re-check; and a fresh **High-risk verdict auto-creates a follow-up task** (same shape as the existing manual one, deduped against open tasks).
 - **Command palette entity search.** ✅ The existing ⌘K command palette (screens + quick actions + recently-viewed) now does live substring search over **contacts, deals, and calls** by name, deep-linking straight to that record (reusing the `initialViewId` preselect pattern already used by the follow-up digest and AI Note Taker's auto-open) instead of only jumping to a screen.
 - **Ambient-detection UI redesign.** ✅ The M15 overlay (banner/toast/switch-prompt/live-capture HUD) was restyled from a plain flat card into a glass HUD capsule — Dynamic-Island/Live-Activity inspired: `backdrop-blur-2xl` translucent surface, a subtle top sheen, pill-shaped buttons, and a breathing live-dot using the `.pulse-ring` utility (defined earlier, unused until now). Pure renderer CSS — identical on Mac and Windows. The tray icon itself still uses one static glyph for every state (tooltip text carries the state instead) — unchanged, since a real per-state glyph needs designed icon assets that don't exist in this repo.
-- **M18 — Session health, the audio ring buffer, Windows capture corrected, and the live edge.** ✅ Corrects two wrong diagnoses from an earlier session before building on top of them: **Windows buyer-side capture was never an OS limitation** — it was opening the wrong render endpoint (`eConsole` instead of the `eCommunications` endpoint VoIP apps actually use); and **the "90-second lag" bug is a ratchet, not a spike** — Deepgram's ingest is capped at 1.25x realtime, so a stall costs a multiple of what it stalled, and it never self-heals. **`src/main/session-health/`** (`timeline.ts`, `lag.ts`, `queue.ts`, `liveness.ts`, `drift.ts`) adds a two-cursor lag metric (submitted vs. acknowledged), a bounded-in-seconds audio queue with silence-first eviction, a rising-slope watchdog that catches the ratchet while absolute lag still looks small, a liveness watchdog (capture-dead / socket-dead / silent, three independent clocks because they fail independently), and drift tracking — all reproduced and proven fixed against a real mock Deepgram server enforcing the 1.25x cap (`lag-regression.test.ts`), not against an idealized one. **The audio ring buffer (§1.4, `features/live/audio/ring.ts` + `audio-pump.worker.ts` + `pump.ts`):** the worklet now writes PCM into a `SharedArrayBuffer`; a dedicated Worker drains it on its own thread and streams straight to the main process over a `MessagePortMain`, so a renderer render-storm or long GC pause can no longer queue audio behind it — falls back to the original postMessage path byte-for-byte whenever anything in the chain is missing, and `--diagnose` reports which route audio actually took rather than which one it was supposed to. **Windows capture:** `switch-policy.ts` auto-switches from per-process to whole-device loopback when a live audio meter and total digital silence coincide (the exact shape of both the Microsoft Teams process-loopback bug and the headset-as-communications-device bug), and a zero-native-code mitigation (`buyer-silence.ts`) detects and surfaces the latter with a one-click deep-link to Windows sound settings — the real fix (a per-process-loopback native addon) is designed and documented (`docs/windows-capture.md`) but deliberately **not written blind**: it cannot be compiled, tested, or reach Windows CI while repo pushes are rejected with a 403, and shipping hundreds of lines of unbuildable COM code would only be honestly describable as "unverified." **The live edge (§4, 8 features):** a screen-share-safe glass-HUD cue rail with a two-tier architecture (`useLiveCues.ts`'s `tierFor` keeps every LLM-derived cue off the interrupt path — only the deterministic "pace" cue may interrupt, proven exhaustively over every `CueKind`); deterministic battlecards (32-card starter library, `<0.2ms` p95 match cost, measured); a question-not-asked checklist; a monologue/talk-ratio meter; an instant post-call brief + follow-up email placed on the clipboard from the main process (so it works while the app has no focus); a commitment extractor (who promised what, split rep vs. prospect); plain-English custom trackers ("tell me when someone mentions procurement" → a working trigger, no admin console); and an always-on-time cue-latency instrumentation (`cue-latency.ts`, nearest-rank p50/p95/max). **Security hardening (§5.3):** Electron fuses locked (`RunAsNode` off closes the `ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE` code-execution escape hatch that would otherwise inherit the app's own mic grant), CSP shipped as a real response header (not just a `<meta>` tag, which silently drops `frame-ancestors`), a durable consent gate (`consent-gate.ts` — capture cannot start without a consent record on disk, re-sanitized on every write **and** read, 20 unit tests including hand-edited-file attacks), and an auto-updater built to default-deny (no feed configured = no network activity at all, guards against the 2020 Doyensec update-bypass class of bug). Also, mid-milestone: an **"always allow recording the other party"** Settings option (records a real `pre-agreed` consent rather than bypassing one — `sanitizeConsent`'s hard invariant stays intact — off by default since only the rep can know they have standing legal basis); a fixed auto-start latch bug that made every detected call after the first silently do nothing; an earned-not-assumed startup interstitial (no more flash of a mic-permission screen that was never true); and a fix so an **auto-started call now actually ends and saves itself** (ambient detection's end-of-call signal was never wired to the thing that stops capture; the app-name-only detector had no end signal at all, so it now times out after 5 minutes of transcribed silence). **`--diagnose`** (§6) prints one block of text — version, platform, capture path, a channel self-test (a known tone per channel through the real interleaver, catching a rep/buyer channel swap that would otherwise silently mislabel a transcript), live session health, consent state, updater trust, and API-key presence — designed so a tester can paste one block instead of the app needing a live call to debug. **Verification:** `docs/session-health.md`, `docs/windows-capture.md`, `docs/security-audit.md`, and `docs/screen-share-safety.md` each separate what's actually been checked from what still needs a live service, a packaged build, or real hardware this dev environment doesn't have; `docs/acceptance-criteria.md` walks all 13 of the milestone's acceptance criteria with real measured numbers (not assertions) for the 7 fully verifiable in-container, including a `worker_threads`-based proof that a genuinely separate OS thread keeps running through a real 5-second synchronous stall on another thread (248/250 ticks landed inside the blocked window) — and honestly defers the rest (real Windows hardware, a live Supabase project, a live LLM key, two machines for cross-app screen-share verification) rather than guessing. A follow-up adversarial bug-hunt (19 agents) found 12 real issues in the new ring/pump path and elsewhere; 11 were fixed (a worker crash after handshake going silently unhandled, a leaked late-arriving `MessagePort`, stale UI banners that could stack contradictorily, `transcription:captureLost` being emitted but never wired to anything, plus two pre-existing bugs in Tasks/Deals where navigating away during the 6-second undo window silently cancelled the delete instead of completing it).
+- **M18 — Session health, the audio ring buffer, Windows capture corrected, and the live edge.** ✅ Corrects two wrong diagnoses from an earlier session before building on top of them: **Windows buyer-side capture was never an OS limitation** — it was opening the wrong render endpoint (`eConsole` instead of the `eCommunications` endpoint VoIP apps actually use); and **the "90-second lag" bug is a ratchet, not a spike** — Deepgram's ingest is capped at 1.25x realtime, so a stall costs a multiple of what it stalled, and it never self-heals. **`src/main/session-health/`** (`timeline.ts`, `lag.ts`, `queue.ts`, `liveness.ts`, `drift.ts`) adds a two-cursor lag metric (submitted vs. acknowledged), a bounded-in-seconds audio queue with silence-first eviction, a rising-slope watchdog that catches the ratchet while absolute lag still looks small, a liveness watchdog (capture-dead / socket-dead / silent, three independent clocks because they fail independently), and drift tracking — all reproduced and proven fixed against a real mock Deepgram server enforcing the 1.25x cap (`lag-regression.test.ts`), not against an idealized one. **The audio ring buffer (§1.4, `features/live/audio/ring.ts` + `audio-pump.worker.ts` + `pump.ts`):** the worklet now writes PCM into a `SharedArrayBuffer`; a dedicated Worker drains it on its own thread and streams straight to the main process over a `MessagePortMain`, so a renderer render-storm or long GC pause can no longer queue audio behind it — falls back to the original postMessage path byte-for-byte whenever anything in the chain is missing, and `--diagnose` reports which route audio actually took rather than which one it was supposed to. **Windows capture:** `switch-policy.ts` auto-switches from per-process to whole-device loopback when a live audio meter and total digital silence coincide (the exact shape of both the Microsoft Teams process-loopback bug and the headset-as-communications-device bug), and a zero-native-code mitigation (`buyer-