From f535faa9bc9814c226a78760c4cefce789c8b236 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 05:53:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 34/39] test: close the six test-coverage gaps from the
 further-improvements sweep
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

143 new tests across backup-core.ts, google-sync.ts, deals-fs.ts,
deal-stages.ts, coach.ts, and deals/staleness.ts — plus a vitest.config.ts fix
(the '@renderer' alias wasn't registered for plain vitest, only for the real
Vite/electron-vite build, so any test that transitively imports a file using
it would have failed to resolve).

Two real bugs found while writing these, both fixed:

- deals-fs.ts: sanitizeDealRecord never read stageHistory back from disk, so
  the field was silently dropped on every load — updateDealUnlocked always
  saw an empty history (because it calls getDeal() first), meaning every
  stage transition after the first silently erased the ones before it. The
  contact-timeline feature this powers was therefore never actually showing
  more than the single most recent transition, no matter how many times a
  deal changed stages. Added sanitizeStageHistory and wired it in.

- deal-stages.ts: setDealStages([]) could never actually return its
  documented 'empty' rejection — sanitizeStageList's "never return an empty
  pipeline" fallback (correct for reading a possibly-corrupt file) ran BEFORE
  the emptiness check, so an empty submission silently became the six
  default stages instead of being rejected. Split into sanitizeStageListRaw
  (pure, may be empty) for setDealStages' own check, keeping the defaulting
  variant for the read/pull paths that actually need it.

coach.ts: exported computeMetrics, makeVerifier, makeFreeTextScrubber, and
normalize (previously module-private) so the deterministic metrics and the
rep-speaker evidence-verifier — the two pieces this whole coaching feature's
grounding claims rest on — can be tested directly rather than only indirectly
through the full AI-calling coachCall().
